{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel, the entire congregation, came to the wilderness of Zin</b> — \"The congregation\" means a complete congregation, for those who were to die in the wilderness had already died, and these were destined for life — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 20:1). And such is the opinion of R. Abraham Ibn Ezra. But if so, what need is there to mention this again when they came afterward to Mount Hor (Numbers 20:22)? And R. Abraham Ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 20:22) that Scripture mentioned this because Edom came out to fight against them, so Scripture noted that not a single person was missing from among them when they came from the city of Edom. But this is not correct, for Israel turned away from him and he did not fight against them at all. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is the practice of Scripture to mention this in the context of complaints: \"And the whole congregation of the children of Israel came to the wilderness of Sin, which is between Elim and Sinai\" (Exodus 16:1), \"And the whole congregation of the children of Israel journeyed according to their stages and camped in Rephidim\" (Exodus 17:1). Scripture informs us that all of them were involved in complaint, and likewise, \"And the whole congregation lifted up their voice\" (Numbers 14:1), \"And the whole congregation of the children of Israel complained on the next day\" (Numbers 17:6). And Scripture said the same when they came to Mount Hor, to inform that all of them were present at the mourning of Aaron the holy one of YHWH, as it says, \"And all the house of Israel wept for Aaron\" (Numbers 20:29), and it says, \"Before the eyes of the whole congregation\" (Numbers 20:27). And in Midrash Numbers Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 19:9) I saw that the expression \"a complete congregation,\" etc., is mentioned only in the second instance, at Mount Hor, while the first instance is because of the complaint, as I have explained." "And the sense of <b>and the people stayed in Kadesh</b> — is to say that when they entered the wilderness of Zin as far as Kadesh, Miriam died. And R. Abraham Ibn Ezra erred when he said that it was because they stayed there many days, for so it is written, since the Kadesh mentioned in the verse: \"And you stayed in Kadesh many days, like the days that you stayed\" (Deuteronomy 1:46) — that is Kadesh-barnea, and it is in the wilderness of Paran, and from there the spies were sent in the second year, and there they returned. But this Kadesh is in the wilderness of Zin, and they arrived there in the fortieth year, and there Miriam died. And the verses are explicitly clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'הרים עליהם' near the end, I read as 'came out against them,' understanding the subject to be the king of Edom based on the context of Ibn Ezra's interpretation being refuted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:04.646813201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}